{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"update-stage30.json\", \"summary\": \"Final item for the run's remaining budget: the observation delivery contract, recorded as a behaviour plus an explained investigation with no defect claim (valid:true).\\n\\nWhat the captured code provides: an observation carries the emitting Raft instance and a documented payload type (vote request, Raft state, peer change, leader change, heartbeat-failure/resume). Delivery is per observer and best-effort by default — `observe` builds one observation per registered observer, applies that observer's filter, skips observers with no channel, and then either sends unconditionally when the observer opted into blocking mode or attempts a non-blocking send and increments a per-observer dropped counter when the channel is full (observer.go:120-149). Registration and deregistration take the observers write lock (observer.go:104-118) while delivery holds only the read lock.\\n\\nThe consequences worth keeping for a consumer: with the default non-blocking mode, losing observations under load is expected and the drop counter is the signal that it happened; with the opt-in blocking mode, the observer can stall whichever internal goroutine emitted the observation — main loop, replication or FSM thread depending on the event — and it holds the read lock for the duration of its send, which blocks registrations but not other observers' delivery. The observation stream is therefore not a durable log. No defect is claimed: the interface promises delivery when an event occurs and the counters make the best-effort semantics observable rather than hidden. Limitations state that this is source-only, that no observer was registered, dropped or blocked in this run, that the blast radius per emitting goroutine was reasoned rather than measured, and that the set of producers was not enumerated exhaustively.\"}"}}
